WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:05.000
On November 4th, 2008, the people
of California voted to fund this:

00:00:05.902 --> 00:00:07.879
A high-speed rail line.

00:00:07.879 --> 00:00:10.938
Many countries around the world have
had high-speed trains for decades.

00:00:10.938 --> 00:00:14.304
And this would be the US's attempt 
to finally catch up.

00:00:14.304 --> 00:00:19.076
The train would whisk passengers from 
LA to San Francisco in under 3 hours.

00:00:19.076 --> 00:00:21.721
And it was all set to open in 2020.

00:00:21.721 --> 00:00:23.734
Today, it's 2022.

00:00:23.734 --> 00:00:27.110
And California's high speed rail project 
is famous.

00:00:27.110 --> 00:00:28.305
For being a disaster.

00:00:28.305 --> 00:00:30.650
"...will be the most expensive project in
state history..."

00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:31.983
"...a train that's going to nowhere..."

00:00:31.983 --> 00:00:32.866
"...train to nowhere..."

00:00:32.866 --> 00:00:36.226
All that's there today is this one section,
still under construction

00:00:36.226 --> 00:00:38.425
from Bakersfield to Merced.

00:00:38.425 --> 00:00:42.175
But the failure of this rail line isn't just
California's problem.

00:00:42.175 --> 00:00:45.952
It's an ominous sign for big projects 
all over the US.

00:00:45.952 --> 00:00:49.641
We see other advanced economies all around
the world that are able to do this.

00:00:49.641 --> 00:00:51.676
So they can do it. So why can't we?

00:00:51.676 --> 00:00:55.898
In other words, what is it about the US that
made California's high-speed rail line

00:00:55.898 --> 00:00:57.554
so hard to build?

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:02.839
Just to be clear; this was a pretty good idea.

00:01:02.839 --> 00:01:06.036
A lot of people travel between 
San Francisco and LA.

00:01:06.036 --> 00:01:08.931
But the trip takes at least 6 hours by car.

00:01:08.931 --> 00:01:10.680
It’s less than an hour by plane

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:14.237
but that’s not counting time in two
of the country’s busiest airports.

00:01:14.237 --> 00:01:18.177
So a 2-hour and 40 minute trip
by high-speed rail made sense.

00:01:18.177 --> 00:01:22.160
California just needed to design a route
that connected its big cities efficiently.

00:01:22.686 --> 00:01:24.280
It's really about population density

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:28.878
and how quickly you can serve areas
of high population density.

00:01:28.878 --> 00:01:30.000
This is Ethan Elkind

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.030
director for the climate program 
at UC Berkeley Law School

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:35.550
and host of the local “State of the Bay”
radio show.

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:36.910
You really want those centers

00:01:36.910 --> 00:01:39.080
where there's a lot of people 
working and living

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:41.670
within a few kilometer range
of the station.

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:45.240
If you don't have that, then it's not worth
a high speed rail stop.

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:48.755
But that’s easier said than done.

00:01:48.755 --> 00:01:51.500
In the early 2000s, the California High-Speed
Rail Authority

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:54.194
considered two routes in Southern California.

00:01:54.194 --> 00:01:56.350
This one went straight up the I-5 highway.

00:01:56.350 --> 00:01:59.480
While this one, looped eastward and stopped
in Palmdale.

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:03.869
It was 34 miles longer and studies estimated
it would be 12 minutes slower.

00:02:03.869 --> 00:02:06.961
Yet, ultimately, this was the route they chose.

00:02:06.961 --> 00:02:08.225
So what happened?

00:02:08.225 --> 00:02:11.319
So the Palmdale stop was really added at the
behest of

00:02:11.319 --> 00:02:13.510
the Los Angeles County Board of Supervisors

00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:16.730
that wanted to see a stop for high speed rail
in Palmdale.

00:02:16.730 --> 00:02:20.285
If you have the county that the high-speed
rail line is going through opposing it

00:02:20.285 --> 00:02:21.980
that can create real political problems

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:25.900
can create litigation problems,
can create permitting problems.

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:30.370
Palmdale politicians understandably wanted
the train’s riders and business opportunities

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:33.060
to come to their district.

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:37.668
The problem was that they had the power to
hold up the entire project to do so.

00:02:37.668 --> 00:02:40.840
They have this power because the US government
gives it to them.

00:02:41.516 --> 00:02:43.081
The US is a federal system

00:02:43.081 --> 00:02:46.321
meaning power is divided between the
federal government and state governments

00:02:46.321 --> 00:02:49.330
which in turn grant some power 
to local governments.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:53.060
When it comes to infrastructure, a lot of
the power and responsibility

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:55.235
is often on this local level.

00:02:55.235 --> 00:02:57.799
That's the compromise that we make here in
the United States.

00:02:57.799 --> 00:03:03.029
We believe in local control to some extent,
you know, representative democracy.

00:03:03.029 --> 00:03:07.489
The downside of that is when you're trying to
get a project at this scale built, you know,

00:03:07.489 --> 00:03:09.195
it does take a lot of compromise.

00:03:09.195 --> 00:03:12.739
And in order to build a 1300-kilometer 
high-speed rail line

00:03:12.739 --> 00:03:15.219
the State High-Speed Rail Authority had to
compromise

00:03:15.219 --> 00:03:17.659
with a lot of local governments.

00:03:17.659 --> 00:03:21.203
In the Bay Area, politicians pushed for this
route, instead of this one,

00:03:21.203 --> 00:03:26.052
even though it was much more expensive and
slower for travelers coming from San Francisco.

00:03:26.052 --> 00:03:29.109
And in the Central Valley, the route stops
in all these cities

00:03:29.109 --> 00:03:31.299
instead going up this faster route

00:03:31.299 --> 00:03:34.200
in order to ensure support 
from the politicians here.

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:39.151
All of these little compromises began adding
up, making the route slower.

00:03:39.151 --> 00:03:40.765
And more expensive.

00:03:40.765 --> 00:03:41.832
Which was a problem.

00:03:41.832 --> 00:03:44.872
Because the funding was already on shaky ground.

00:03:46.192 --> 00:03:48.099
When California voted in 2008

00:03:48.099 --> 00:03:52.805
the state estimated it would need
$33 billion to complete by 2020.

00:03:52.805 --> 00:03:57.841
But voters were told that California taxpayers
would only have to pay 9 billion.

00:03:57.841 --> 00:04:00.189
That’s because the planners were counting
on the federal government

00:04:00.189 --> 00:04:02.782
to chip in $12-16 billion.

00:04:02.782 --> 00:04:04.387
And it made some sense:

00:04:04.387 --> 00:04:08.410
The federal government spends tens of billions
of dollars a year on transportation infrastructure.

00:04:09.334 --> 00:04:13.094
The problem was only a fraction of it goes
to trains.

00:04:13.094 --> 00:04:15.900
Partly because, unlike most countries with
high-speed rail

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:19.546
the US has a stark political divide on whether
we even need it.

00:04:21.344 --> 00:04:24.400
By 2010, the Democrat-controlled Congress
had allocated

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:27.410
just over $3 billion for the project.

00:04:27.410 --> 00:04:28.278
Not nothing,

00:04:28.278 --> 00:04:30.678
but not nearly as much as the state wanted.

00:04:31.879 --> 00:04:35.919
But by 2014, the Democrats had lost control
of Congress to the Republicans

00:04:35.919 --> 00:04:39.168
who opposed this kind of mass-transit project.

00:04:39.168 --> 00:04:41.979
"More money for California’s 
high-speed rail."

00:04:41.979 --> 00:04:44.641
"I call it high-cost rail, is a terrible idea."

00:04:44.641 --> 00:04:47.639
"None of these funds can be used 
for high speed rail."

00:04:47.639 --> 00:04:51.503
They made it very difficult for any further
federal money to reach this project

00:04:51.503 --> 00:04:53.661
leaving it well short of funding.

00:04:53.661 --> 00:04:55.732
It’s an example of how, in the US

00:04:55.732 --> 00:04:59.930
long-term projects can be at the mercy of
whichever party is in power,

00:04:59.930 --> 00:05:03.463
since they have often have the power 
to stall it at any time.

00:05:03.463 --> 00:05:06.210
And it's not just the federal government.

00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:09.331
Private citizens can also hold things up.

00:05:11.211 --> 00:05:13.210
It took less than a year for people 
to start suing

00:05:13.210 --> 00:05:15.509
the California high-speed rail project.

00:05:15.509 --> 00:05:19.699
Many cases were based on a law called
the California Environmental Quality Act.

00:05:19.699 --> 00:05:21.310
Known as CEQA.

00:05:21.310 --> 00:05:22.840
CEQA requires the government

00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:26.050
to study the environmental effects of any
government project,

00:05:26.050 --> 00:05:28.924
explore alternatives, and release the findings
in a report.

00:05:28.924 --> 00:05:33.360
It's very easy to find a hole in that assessment
and then file a lawsuit.

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:35.690
It's essentially target practice for lawyers.

00:05:35.690 --> 00:05:38.331
Many of these lawsuits had legitimate concerns.

00:05:38.331 --> 00:05:40.140
Some farmers in the Central Valley

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:42.620
worried about the train damaging their agricultural land

00:05:42.620 --> 00:05:44.784
irrigation systems, and crops.

00:05:44.784 --> 00:05:49.780
But others, simply used CEQA to try and keep
the project from being built in their neighborhood.

00:05:49.780 --> 00:05:53.540
This is a common way in the US for private
citizens to block important projects

00:05:53.540 --> 00:05:55.780
like housing or infrastructure.

00:05:56.377 --> 00:05:58.241
It's not cheap to bring a CEQA lawsuit.

00:05:58.241 --> 00:06:03.190
Sometimes you've got very powerful homeowners
groups that have sued and force changes or

00:06:03.190 --> 00:06:05.360
at least delayed the project and run up costs.

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:08.363
For the high-speed rail line, these lawsuits
were like roadblocks.

00:06:08.363 --> 00:06:12.760
And to clear them, the state had to hire legions
of expensive lawyers.

00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:14.530
Which added time and costs.

00:06:14.530 --> 00:06:17.090
I think you do want to have 
a participatory system.

00:06:17.090 --> 00:06:19.800
You do want to have people able 
to say their piece

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:21.948
and you want policymakers to respond.

00:06:21.948 --> 00:06:23.456
Just that you don't want to get to the point

00:06:23.456 --> 00:06:27.270
where a hyper-local interest has veto 
over a project

00:06:27.270 --> 00:06:29.734
of really statewide importance.

00:06:30.509 --> 00:06:35.570
But take away local politicians, and federal
drama, and lawsuits, and this project would’ve

00:06:35.570 --> 00:06:37.539
still faced problems.

00:06:38.459 --> 00:06:41.080
The state body in charge of designing and
managing this project

00:06:41.080 --> 00:06:43.660
was the California High-Speed Rail Authority.

00:06:43.660 --> 00:06:45.280
There was just one problem.

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:48.240
The high speed rail authority had never built
anything like this.

00:06:48.471 --> 00:06:49.339
Whoops.

00:06:49.339 --> 00:06:51.690
Obviously it's a tried and true technology
globally

00:06:51.690 --> 00:06:54.229
but it was new in North America 
when California tried it.

00:06:54.229 --> 00:06:57.791
Certainly we didn't have the competence in-
house how to build it here.

00:06:57.791 --> 00:07:00.964
When Japan and France were building their
high-speed rail networks

00:07:00.964 --> 00:07:04.801
they had legions of experienced engineers
inside their governments.

00:07:04.801 --> 00:07:09.240
But the California High-Speed Rail Authority
had to hire consultants and contractors to

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:11.560
handle the design and construction.

00:07:11.560 --> 00:07:15.960
These consultants were four times the cost
in some cases of what it would have cost to

00:07:15.960 --> 00:07:18.030
hire someone just to do it in-house.

00:07:18.030 --> 00:07:22.733
Another reason why the Authority had to keep
increasing the estimated cost of the project.

00:07:22.733 --> 00:07:27.412
Which today, is up to $113 billion 
to complete the whole line.

00:07:27.412 --> 00:07:30.599
But, the state only has enough money to build
this section

00:07:30.599 --> 00:07:32.886
in the rural Central Valley.

00:07:32.886 --> 00:07:35.389
Even if it can get the rest, 
it will probably take

00:07:35.389 --> 00:07:38.189
a few more decades to finish this project.

00:07:40.069 --> 00:07:44.518
High-speed rail is a cautionary tale for really
big transformative projects in the US.

00:07:44.518 --> 00:07:47.669
We need all sorts of rail, transit, bus lanes,

00:07:47.669 --> 00:07:50.389
new renewable energy facilities, all the things
we need to do

00:07:50.389 --> 00:07:53.881
to have a more sustainable and vibrant
economy.

00:07:53.881 --> 00:07:57.342
But the US is falling further and further
behind its peers.

00:07:57.342 --> 00:08:01.358
Because the political compromises, under-funding,
lawsuits,

00:08:01.358 --> 00:08:04.360
and extra costs that hampered California's
high-speed rail

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:07.150
can also happen to any other big project the
US takes on.

